---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { manifest } from '../lib/data';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method & limits — Round Table"
  description="Provenance, public-data rules, ground-truth caveats, and known limitations for the Round Table simulation showcase."
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-[var(--border)]">
    <div class="fog"></div>
    <div class="page-shell relative py-14 md:py-20">
      <span class="eyebrow">Method &amp; limits</span>
      <h1 class="display mt-4 max-w-4xl text-[clamp(2.6rem,6vw,4.6rem)]">
        Real output. Limited claims.<br /><span class="text-gold">Clear provenance.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede mt-5 max-w-3xl">
        This site exists to show how the framework behaves, not to make claims
        about the real people whose names appear in the simulations. Every
        transcript is model-generated fiction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="page-shell prose-custom max-w-4xl py-14 md:py-20">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      Round Table is an unofficial, non-commercial research project. It is not
      affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to <em>The Traitors</em>, its
      producers, its broadcasters, or any contestant. No photographs or
      likenesses are used. Cast sigils are generated from a hash of the player id.
    </p>
    <p>
      The personas are simplified behavioural sketches written from publicly
      broadcast episodes. They are not psychological profiles, and nothing on this
      site should be read as a statement about a real person's character,
      intentions, or conduct.
    </p>

    <h2>Data provenance</h2>
    <p>
      The Python project records each run as a directory under
      <code>agent/runs/&lt;game_id&gt;/</code>: config, event log, transcript,
      metrics, per-call telemetry, and, for season runs, a benchmark and
      diagnosis. Those directories are local artifacts and are never committed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The website is built from <code>web/src/data/</code>, a sanitized JSON export
      produced by <code>web/tools/export_site_data.py</code>. The exporter is the
      only path from the run artifacts to the site. It reads the simulation package
      as a library and writes summaries, round digests, flagship transcripts, and
      season ground truth.
    </p>
    <p>
      {manifest.totals.runs} completed runs are published: {manifest.totals.playerGames}
      player-games, {manifest.totals.messages.toLocaleString('en')} messages, and
      {manifest.totals.calls.toLocaleString('en')} model calls. Three runs have a
      full transcript; the rest are summary-plus-metrics.
    </p>

    <h2>What is published</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Outcomes, casts, roles, elimination order, votes, nominations, councils, recruits, and items.</li>
      <li>Model-generated dialogue for the three flagship runs, verbatim, including code-switching and simulated conflict.</li>
      <li>Deterministic quality metrics: hallucination, duplication, secrecy, parsing, private-chat reciprocity, and speech similarity.</li>
      <li>Token and call counts, prompt sizes, retries, and failure classes.</li>
      <li>Season benchmarks against recorded ground truth, with provenance and caveats.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is intentionally omitted</h2>
    <p>
      The repository is public, so the export strips machine-specific detail.
      In particular:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Absolute latency, wall-clock run duration, throughput, and in-flight concurrency. These describe one host on one afternoon, not the framework.</li>
      <li>Endpoint URLs, LAN addresses, and local filesystem paths.</li>
      <li>Model digests, API keys, and raw provider error bodies.</li>
      <li><code>game.db</code>, raw <code>llm_calls.jsonl</code>, and raw <code>events.jsonl</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The exporter and a CI check scan the committed JSON for IPv4 addresses,
      absolute paths, 64-hex digests, key shapes, local hostnames, and forbidden
      keys. A match fails the data check.
    </p>

    <h2>Benchmarks are comparisons, not accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      A season benchmark is a weighted alignment score between a run and a recorded
      season file: outcome, traitor roster, banishment and murder alignment and
      overlap, hit rate, survival curve, final counts, finale, recruitments, and
      exit order. Components with no data on one side are skipped and their weight
      is redistributed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some season files are marked unverified in their headers. The site shows that
      caveat where it applies. A high score means the simulation's event flow is
      closer to the recorded season; it does not mean the simulation is accurate
      about the people or the show's production.
    </p>

    <h2>Known limits</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Template collapse.</strong> Late-game prompts are mostly transcript.
      Models copy what they can see, and repeated phrasing is a measured property
      of the run, not a bug that disappears when ignored.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>End-vote disposition.</strong> Faithful players still lean toward
      ending the game even when a traitor is alive. The hard-coded prompt bias was
      removed, but the model's disposition remains and is measured.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Small samples.</strong> One run is one story. The ledger, holdout
      seeds, and paired gates exist because a single win rate is not evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Structural visibility is not the whole prompt.</strong> The boundary
      explorer shows channel membership. The real prompt also carries the player's
      role knowledge, memory, beliefs, relationships, and a bounded transcript
      window.
    </p>

    <h2>Content note</h2>
    <p>
      The transcripts are generated by language models playing a competitive social
      game. They can contain insults, accusations, manipulation, and strong
      language. They are fiction produced by the simulation, not statements by the
      people whose names are used as character labels.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      The source, configs, and full documentation are in the
      <a href="https://github.com/kweusuf/traitors-simulation-using-agents" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub repository</a>.
      Issues and corrections are welcome there.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
